## Why This Is Asked
Handles a realistic, repeatable issue in multi-tenant Linux environments. Evaluates practical debugging, safe isolation, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- inotify and max_user_watches
- per-tenant resource control (ulimit, cgroups)
- observability and test planning
- safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple Node.js watcher demo illustrating per-tenant file activity
const fs = require('fs');
const path = '/ci-scratch/tenant-A';
fs.watch(path, {recursive: true}, (ev, f) => {
  console.log(`[watch] ${ev}: ${f}`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate per-tenant limit adjustments and verification?
- How would you validate rollback in a production window without impacting running builds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates end-to-end diagnosis and mitigation of TLS handshake tail latency in multi-tenant Linux environments under production-like load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- TLS performance optimization, session resumption, and CPU pinning
- Hardware crypto offload and the kernel crypto API
- eBPF observability with per-tenant accounting
- Canary rollouts, staged rollouts, and rollback procedures

## Code Example
```bash
# Trace TLS handshake events with BPF (conceptual)
bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:tls:handshake {
  @tenant_id = comm;
  @latency = hist(nsecs - args->start_time);
}'
```
